  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1200 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThursday night through Sunday:\n\nA period of dry conditions should linger Thursday night into\nFriday, with southeast winds and continued warm temperatures.\n500 mb cluster analysis continues to support a ridge lingering\nover the Great Lakes region, which should help with keeping the\nmild temperatures in the area. Ensemble members also show good\nagreement with this. \n\nAnother low pressure system should move northeast from the \ncentral High Plains into Ontario Friday into Saturday night, \nwith a cold front moving east across the region. This is \nsupported with decent agreement of the low track by ensemble \nmeans. Ensemble members are showing at least some light \nprecipitation with the frontal passage on Saturday into Saturday\nnight, though there are some members that are not as robust \nwith precipitation. A few storms may occur during this period, \ndepending on if high enough dew points move in and some \ninstability can develop. There is pretty strong deep layer shear\nahead of and along the front, so thunder potential will be \nsomething to watch.\n\nFor now, kept the NBM 30 to 60 percent chances for showers \nmainly Saturday into Saturday night. These chances will likely \nfluctuate more as this period draws closer. Ensembles are then\ntrending cooler to more seasonable temperatures behind the \nfront for Sunday into early next week. \n\nWood",
